          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows a present loving emotional state. The narration explicitly characterizes the utterance as an honest admission.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression avows love but does not itself undertake a promise, obligation, future course of action, or defined relationship. No commitment from nearby dialogue should be transferred onto it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"Besides functioning as an avowal, the expression is represented as a sudden, partly unrestrained affective discharge: it spills out before Nikki can stop it and feels like a release.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the truth-presenting avowal and its affective-release quality is supported. The utterance is not framed as a question, request, command, greeting, or other distinct act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Nikki says this suddenly to Keith while they are lying together after an intimate encounter. The narration presents her as emotionally full and the words as escaping before deliberation. Keith pauses, smiles, reciprocates, and they kiss.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Nikki undertakes no specific obligation or future action through the target expression. Keith clearly receives it as an admission of love and reciprocates with “I love you too,” but that reciprocal avowal does not by itself establish a promise or defined relational commitment by either person.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize a specific published work from the supplied passage.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented affection and immediate uptake, but it does not establish precise relationship terms, durable intentions, or any commitment created by the target utterance.\"}"
        }
